<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tran Quoc Pagoda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Trấn Quốc Pagoda, Khai Quoc Pagoda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tran Quoc Pagoda in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> is regarded as the oldest Buddhist temple in the city. Tradition says it was founded in the 6th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-nam-de">Ly Nam De</a> as the Khai Quoc pagoda, on the bank of the Red River. It was moved in 1615 to a small island in West Lake, where it stands today with a tall brick stupa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tran-quoc-pagoda.jpg" alt="Tran Quoc Pagoda" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RB Photo · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tran_Quoc_Pagoda,_Hanoi,_Vietnam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The temple is said to have been built during the reign of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-nam-de">Lý Nam Đế</a>, from 544 to 548, and its first name, Khai Quốc, means National Founding. It stood on the shore of the Red River, outside the Yên Phụ Dyke, and was moved because the river was encroaching on it. Its new site was Kim Ngư, or Golden Fish, islet near the south-eastern shore of West Lake (Hồ Tây), linked to the mainland by a small causeway. Over the years the temple was also called An Quốc, Pacification of the Realm, and Trấn Bắc, Guardian of the North.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The last major repair took place in 1815, when the main sanctuary, the reception hall and the rear hall of the dead were renovated, and the historic relics include statues dating to 1639. The pagoda holds the ashes of important monks. Lotus flowers, symbols of purity and enlightenment, appear in statues and in carvings on a stone wall beside the pagoda. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhi-tree">Bodhi tree</a> on the grounds was grown from a cutting of the tree at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, given in 1959 to mark the visit of the Indian president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-prasad">Rajendra Prasad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Monks have lived at the pagoda for centuries, teaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> to the public and praying at the shrines around the grounds; they are vegetarian, so offerings are made without meat. Visitors burn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> at the main shrine in odd numbers, held to be lucky, and leave food or small sums of money. Shrines at the front of the courtyard honour the Mẫu, or Mothers: the green Mother of mountains and forests, the white Mother of the water and the red Mother of the sky, among the oldest deities worshipped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, there before the Buddha. Worshippers come especially in the first month of the Vietnamese calendar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trấn Quốc Pagoda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ly Nam De set up a court on Chinese lines, with an era name and officials, and built the <b>Khai Quoc</b> pagoda near Long Bien.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-ly-dynasty">Early Ly dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
